    "result_text": "All 271 tests pass. \n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented digits-only telephone storage with normalization applied before the uniqueness check.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`rest/function/owner/Telephones.java`** (new) \u2014 small helper `digitsOnly(String)` that strips everything except digits (handles spaces, dashes, parentheses), returning `null` unchanged.\n\n2. **`rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java`** (POST `/api/owners`) \u2014 normalizes the telephone to digits right after mapping the request into the `Owner`, so the subsequent `checkDuplicate` step (`CheckDuplicateOwner`) compares normalized values. This makes `(613) 555-0100` and `6135550100` collide \u2192 409.\n\n3. **`rest/function/owner/ApplyOwner.java`** (PUT `/api/owners/{ownerId}`) \u2014 normalizes on update too, keeping the stored value digits-only.\n\n4. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 relaxed the `OwnerFields.telephone` bind pattern from `^[0-9]*$` to `^[-0-9 ()]*$` so a punctuated number is accepted at the request edge (previously it was rejected with a 400 before any normalization could run). The `Owner` entity's `@Pattern(^[0-9]{10}$)` still enforces exactly 10 digits on the *stored* (normalized) value.\n\nThe stored digits are returned as-is in the response `telephone` field (`GET` reads back `6135550100`). Since normalization happens in `BuildOwner` before `CheckDuplicateOwner`, and the entity setter is untouched, the domain model stays unchanged per project conventions.\n\nThe `Cp11Tests` (both `coreStoresDigitsOnly` and `functionalityNormalisedDuplicateRejected`) now pass, along with the full suite (271 tests, 0 failures).",
